    height: 910
    caption: Proposed map of an independent "Assyro-Chaldea", presented by the
      Assyrian groups at the Paris Peace Conference in 1919.
  - file: Assyrian_refugees_on_wagon.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Assyrian_refugees_on_wagon.jpg/250px-Assyrian_refugees_on_wagon.jpg
    width: 1356
    height: 1112
    caption: Assyrian refugees in the steppe between the Tigris and Euphrates in 1939
  - file: Project_assyria_(updated).png
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Project_assyria_%28updated%29.png/330px-Project_assyria_%28updated%29.png
    width: 4972
    height: 2517
    caption: The Assyrian people, due to genocide experienced in Turkey, Syria and
      Iran, were drastically reduced in numbers. Later on, many events (civil
      wars in Iraq/Syria, the Turkish/Kurdish Conflict, the Iranian Revolution
      and the emergence of ISIS) all played a role in reducing Assyrian presence
      in the Middle East.Today, more Assyrians live in the diaspora rather than
      in the historic Assyrian homeland.
  - file: Şehîd_Margrêt_Corc,_yekem_pêşmergeya_kurd.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/%C5%9Eeh%C3%AEd_Margr%C3%AAt_Corc%2C_yekem_p%C3%AA%C5%9Fmergeya_kurd.jpg/250px-%C5%9Eeh%C3%AEd_Margr%C3%AAt_Corc%2C_yekem_p%C3%AA%C5%9Fmergeya_kurd.jpg
    width: 1400
    height: 2189
    caption: Margaret George Shello (1942–1969), an iconic Assyrian guerilla fighter
      and military leader of the Kurdish Peshmerga forces in Iraq
